    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d605">

                <pb facs="0693" n="600" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg600"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.1431/116: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >O’Donoghue</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-10T19:00:00-04:00">July 10, 1935—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>22. With reference to the last paragraph of your telegram No. 34 of July 8, 5
                    p.m., Article VI of the general provisions prohibits reciprocally application of
                    quotas, subject to exceptions therein defined, on products enumerated in
                    Schedules I and II of the agreement. It would be helpful if Guatemala would
                    submit at once a list of its exports to the United States on which it desires
                    continued free entry. As outlined in the report transmitted to you under cover
                    of the Department’s instruction No. 149 of February 8, 1935,<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d605-f1"/> this Government is prepared to give such a commitment on
                    coffee and bananas. Other requests in this sense that Guatemala may make will be
                    considered sympathetically. Such commitments, however, are made only to chief or
                    important suppliers of this market.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
